hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"385","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who said: Narrated to us Rawḥ, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Uthman ibn al-Sa'ib informed me, from Umm 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, from Abu Mahdhurah.\n\nAnd it was also narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Rafi', who said: Narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Uthman ibn al-Sa'ib, the freed slave of their people, informed me from his father, the freed slave of Abu Mahdhurah, and from Umm 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, that they both heard this from Abu Mahdhurah.\n\nAnd it was also narrated to us by Yazid ibn Sinan, who said: Narrated to us Abu 'Asim, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Uthman ibn al-Sa'ib informed me, from his father and from Umm 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, from Abu Mahdhurah. This is the narration of al-Dawraqi.\n\nHe said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) returned from Hunayn, ten of us from Makkah went out in search of him. We heard them giving the call to prayer, so we stood up and gave the call to prayer mocking them. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"I heard a human giving a beautiful call to prayer among these people.\" So he sent for us, and we gave the call to prayer one by one. I was the last of them. When I gave the call to prayer, he said: \"Come here.\" He made me sit before him, wiped his hand over my forehead, and blessed me three times. Then he said: \"Go and give the call to prayer at the Sacred House.\"\n\nI said: \"How, O Messenger of Allah?\" So he taught me the call to prayer as it is now given:\n\n*\"Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar (Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest),\nAshhadu an la ilaha illallah (I bear witness that there is no god but Allah),\nAshhadu an la ilaha illallah,\nAshhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah (I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah),\nAshhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah,\nHayya 'ala al-salah (Come to prayer),\nHayya 'ala al-salah,\nHayya 'ala al-falah (Come to success),\nHayya 'ala al-falah,\nAl-salatu khayrun min al-nawm (Prayer is better than sleep),\nAl-salatu khayrun min al-nawm,\nIn the first part of the dawn,\nAllahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar,\nLa ilaha illallah.\"*\n\nAnd he taught me the iqamah twice:\n\n*\"Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar,\nAshhadu an la ilaha illallah,\nAshhadu an la ilaha illallah,\nAshhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah,\nAshhadu anna Muhammadan Rasulullah,\nHayya 'ala al-salah,\nHayya 'ala al-salah,\nHayya 'ala al-falah,\nHayya 'ala al-falah,\nQad qamat al-salah (Prayer is about to begin),\nQad qamat al-salah,\nAllahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar,\nLa ilaha illallah.\"*\n\nIbn Jurayj said: 'Uthman informed me of this entire report from his father and from Umm 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, that she heard it from Abu Mahdhurah.\n\nIbn Rafi' and Yazid ibn Sinan mentioned in the hadith at the beginning of the call to prayer: *\"Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar\"* (Allah is the Greatest four times). Yazid ibn Sinan mentioned the iqamah twice, just as al-Dawraqi did.\n\nIbn Rafi' said in his narration: \"And when you give the iqamah, say it twice: *Qad qamat al-salah, qad qamat al-salah* (Prayer is about to begin, prayer is about to begin). Did you hear?\" And he added: Abu Mahdhurah would not trim his forelock nor part it, because the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had wiped his hand over it.\n\nYazid ibn Sinan added at the end of his narration: Ibn Jurayj said: 'Uthman informed me of this entire report from his father and from Umm 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Mahdhurah, that they both heard it from Abu Mahdhurah."
"386","Narrated by Muhammad bin Othman Al-Ijli, narrated by Abu Usama, on the authority of Ibn Awf, on the authority of Muhammad bin Sirin, on the authority of Anas, who said: “From the Sunnah when he says The muezzin in the dawn call to prayer: He greeted the farmer and said: “Prayer is better than sleep.”"
